---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Bisection Method — CMNA"
  description="The bisection method as implemented in CMNA."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Root finding</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">The bisection method</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Bracket a real root, halve the interval, keep the half that still contains
        the sign change, and repeat.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell two-column">
      <article class="content">
        <h2>The idea</h2>
        <p>
          Suppose a continuous function changes sign between <code>a</code> and
          <code>b</code>. The intermediate value theorem tells us that at least one
          real root lies between them. Bisection repeatedly evaluates the midpoint
          and discards the half-interval that cannot contain the sign change.
        </p>
        <p>
          The method is not glamorous, but it is difficult to misunderstand. Each
          successful iteration cuts the width of the bracket in half. That makes the
          convergence behavior explicit and gives us a direct handle on the remaining
          interval error.
        </p>

        <h2>CMNA implementation</h2>
        <p>
          The companion package stops when the interval width is below
          <code>tol</code>, or when the iteration cap <code>m</code> is exceeded.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="code-panel">
        <pre><code>bisection &lt;- function(f, a, b, tol = 1e-3, m = 100) {
    iter &lt;- 0
    f.a &lt;- f(a)
    f.b &lt;- f(b)

    while (abs(b - a) &gt; tol) {
        iter &lt;- iter + 1
        if (iter &gt; m) {
            warning("iterations maximum exceeded")
            break
        }

        xmid &lt;- (a + b) / 2
        ymid &lt;- f(xmid)

        if (f.a * ymid &gt; 0) {
            a &lt;- xmid
            f.a &lt;- ymid
        } else {
            b &lt;- xmid
            f.b &lt;- ymid
        }
    }

    root &lt;- (a + b) / 2
    return(root)
}</code></pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell content">
      <h2>Why it belongs here</h2>
      <p>
        Bisection is a particularly good model for the CMNA approach because the
        method, the code, and the convergence argument all remain visible at once.
        There is almost no software machinery between the mathematical statement and
        the implementation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/k3jph/cmna-pkg/blob/main/R/bisection.R">View the current source on GitHub →</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
